1: # Plan 6: prepared unbounded support and proven scalar preludes
2: 
3: Status: proposed for two fresh adversarial CLI reviews. Do not implement before
4: folding surviving compiler and execution findings into this plan. The command
5: automasking gate is complete. This slice handles nonanchored unbounded commands;
6: anchored grab, preview, dyntopo, general attributes and other host hooks remain
7: explicit subsequent dependencies of the full Plan 6 gate.
8: 
9: ## Current implementation constraints
10: 
11: - Kelvinlet is both unbounded and grab-capable. Anchored mode chooses
12:   AccumOrigGrab and requires first-touch image arbitration; this slice keeps the
13:   anchored guard. Nonanchored Kelvinlet still has a generated host clamp and a
14:   scalar reduce prelude, so merely removing the unbounded guard is insufficient.
15: - The compiler marks every host/reduce stage unsafe. Its assignment checker only
16:   admits lexical value locals and direct vertex geometry writes. Kelvinlet's
17:   host writes mu/nu, whose exact declared domains already guarantee those clamps
18:   are no-ops after successful prepared scalar validation. Its reduce computes
19:   two scalar outputs from those values without touching shared state.
20: - An unbounded field has an optional cutoff at radius * unboundedExtent. Extent
21:   <= 0 disables the cutoff. A conservative all-leaf query is correct for both;
22:   a radius-only query truncates later unbounded stages. Cavity first contact must
23:   cover the field's actual support, rather than the regular brush sphere.
24: 
25: ## Implementation
26: 
27: 1. Add conservative compiler proofs for two narrowly defined preludes.
28:    A host body qualifies only when it contains canonical scalar-uniform range
29:    clamps: `if (field > declared_max) field = declared_max` or the corresponding
30:    lower-bound form, with no else, extra writes, unknown calls, loops or context
31:    access. Restrict the initial proof to FLOAT32 fields with explicit finite
32:    ranges, matching comparison/assignment constants exactly. Normalize domains
33:    with the same representable-bound rules used at preparation. All other host
34:    stages remain ineligible. Preserve original host code for raw execution and
35:    retain its call during prepared execution, where the proof guarantees no change.
36: 2. A reduce prelude qualifies only for straight-line scalar local/output
37:    assignments from literals, current scalar uniforms, already initialized locals
38:    or earlier initialized outputs, using pure arithmetic expressions. Require
39:    every output initialized before use and before completion. Reject shared writes,
40:    input-parameter writes, unknown calls, loops, neighbor/geometry/context reads
41:    and ambiguous shadowing. Keep general compiler behavior unchanged; this proof
42:    controls prepared eligibility only. Combine these proofs with the existing
43:    assignment/call audit; never allow a safe prelude to hide unsafe vertex writes.
44:    Emit default-false command metadata for proven no-op host stages and include
45:    proven reduce stages in preparedScalarSafe. Do not whitelist a tool name.
46: 3. Snapshot unboundedExtent independently for every prepared stage, including
47:    stages whose kernel does not declare it, so an earlier declaring command cannot
48:    leak its working value. If the current manifest declares it, use its prepared
49:    typed value. Otherwise inherit the authoritative Brush member without adding
50:    a persistent property or allowing an undeclared scalar override. Apply the
51:    stage snapshot and restore the original working member with existing scopes.
52: 4. After full scalar/metadata validation, validate each positive-radius unbounded
53:    stage's finite extent and finite positive cutoff arithmetic when enabled.
54:    Keep zero-radius stages skipped. A program with any executing unbounded stage
55:    selects all nonempty mesh/grid leaves; bounded-only programs retain the existing
56:    maximum-radius query. Do not mutate query lists, publish, capture, change
57:    topology or allocate persistent attributes on a rejected candidate.
58: 5. Admit proven safe nonanchored unbounded commands in both prepared executors.
59:    Execute the already-prepared command and prelude without legacy loadProps or
60:    a second dynamics evaluation. Cavity contact for an unbounded stage is strictly
61:    inside its positive cutoff, or every visited vertex when cutoff is disabled.
62:    Bounded stage contact remains its own spherical support even in an all-leaf
63:    shared program. Keep the stage column valid outside contact as before.
64: 
65: ## Acceptance
66: 
67: - Compiler positive/negative fixtures cover each permitted syntax, nearby unsafe
68:   variants, shadowing, assignments hidden in index expressions/calls, output
69:   initialization, mixed safe/unsafe stages and built-in Kelvinlet generation.
70:   Existing compiler/backend checks must continue to pass; regenerate native/TS
71:   declarations and generated kernels through the dispatcher.
72: - Mesh live/CSR and grid immediate/deferred tests compare nonanchored standalone
73:   and program Kelvinlet with independent raw execution over all leaves. Include
74:   DRAW followed by a larger unbounded field, changed pressure and material stacks,
75:   zero/missed first dabs, cutoff disabled, exact boundary, growing support, and
76:   subsequent bounded strokes. Use multiple leaves and vertices outside the main
77:   brush's original region. Demonstrate actual movement there.
78: - Test invalid extent/product/late-stage candidates before topology, undo,
79:   counters, properties and query-state mutation. Command extent inheritance and
80:   scoped restoration must survive success, miss, zero radius and rejection.
81: - Cavity-on unbounded stages must match independent cutoff-support oracles and
82:   keep first-contact values for subsequent dabs. Different scalar material values
83:   must not create accidental cavity configuration changes.
84: - Exercise required-prepared C API/Python calls and input batches with the current
85:   nonanchored geometric context. Test native undo/redo and installed package
86:   provenance. Do not claim anchored modal adoption from this bounded slice.
87: 
88: The all-leaf query is a deliberate correctness-first choice for unbounded stages.
89: It avoids support truncation without extending today's pinned-grab lifetime
90: rules. A later measured optimization may use a finite validated extent query.
